<BaseLayout title="Average Reaction Time: Typical Browser Ranges and How to Read Them" description="See what an average reaction time can mean on a five-round visual test. Compare best, median, and average scores without treating one number as a universal ranking.">
  <section class="article-hero"><div class="shell"><p class="eyebrow">Score guide</p><h1>Average Reaction Time</h1><p class="lede">A browser average reaction time is the mean of five valid visual rounds on this site, not a medical ranking or a single number that fits every person.</p></div></section>
  <article class="article-page article-copy">
    <h2>Typical average reaction time on a simple visual click</h2>
    <p>On a simple “wait for green, then click” task, many healthy adults land somewhere near 200–300 ms in lab settings, while browser tests often read slower because of display and input latency. This site’s live score bands treat about 300–350 ms as a common browser-test middle. That is an informal reference for the same five-round visual test, not a clinical cutoff.</p>
    <p>Age, sleep, caffeine, which hand you use, and whether the task is a simple reaction or a choice reaction all move the number. A Formula 1 start-light time and a phone tap are not the same measurement as this page.</p>
    <h2>There is no one browser average that fits every test</h2>
    <p>People often search for an average reaction time after taking a quick test. The useful first question is “average of what?” A response task can ask for one action after one visible signal, require a choice between several signals, ask the user to move a pointer to a target, or require repeated tapping. Those are different tasks, so their recorded times should not be mixed as though they measure the same event.</p>
    <p>Even within a simple visual click test, the reported number is influenced by the device path. The screen must display a change, the user notices it, an input device reports an action, and the browser processes that event. Public browser-test references describe display and input latency as part of that context. This site therefore does not present a universal “normal” score or classify a visitor from one session.</p>
    <h2>What the five-round summary means</h2>
    <h3>Best</h3>
    <p>Your best score is the smallest valid result in the five rounds. It can be satisfying to see, but it is only one observation. Use it to record a personal high point, not as the whole story.</p>
    <h3>Median</h3>
    <p>The median is the middle number after the five scores are sorted from low to high. With five values, it is one of the actual rounds. It is often a helpful center point because one unusually slow or unusually quick click does not move it as much as it moves an average.</p>
    <h3>Average</h3>
    <p>The average adds the five valid results and divides by five. It shows the overall session, including every round. Compare it with the median to notice whether a single outlier changed the feel of the run.</p>
    <h2>Make comparisons that mean something</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Use the same input method each time: mouse, touch, or keyboard.</li>
      <li>Keep the same device and browser when tracking a personal series.</li>
      <li>Complete the full five rounds instead of comparing only one click.</li>
      <li>Repeat a session at a different time rather than treating a single run as a final judgment.</li>
      <li>Note interruptions, fatigue, and changes to your setup before inferring a trend.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>These habits do not turn a web game into a laboratory instrument. They simply make personal comparisons less noisy. Do not use an online reaction test to diagnose a health condition or decide whether you are safe to drive, work, or compete.</p>
